The feature map dialect. A feature map is the top of the chain that turns intent into working code:

| Layer       | Artifact                          | Who writes it            | What it says                         |
| ----------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| Feature map | `FEATURES.mdl`, `feature` blocks  | a person, or an agent    | what the product should do, and why  |
| Spec        | `PLUGIN.mdl`, `SPEC.mdl`          | agent, reviewed in a PR  | how one package meets the feature    |
| Review      | `<SPEC>.review.mdl`, `verdict`s   | agent (spec-review)      | how far the code meets each spec item |
| Tasks       | `TASKS.md` beside the spec        | agent                    | the work that closes each gap        |
| Code        | sources and tests                 | agent                    | the behaviour                        |
| Result      | the re-run review, the feature's status | agent             | that the feature now holds           |

A feature is a goal state ("Feeds drop entries older than their retention"); it names the spec items
that state it and does not restate them. Its status is authored, and `deus features check` derives a
second status from the reviews of the items it cites, so a claim the code does not support shows up
as a warning. The shape matches plugin-projects' `Feature` (`org.dxos.type.feature`), so a map can be
loaded into a project's feature board.

A feature map is a spec: every change adds a decision to its `## Changelog`, so the map records who
asked for each feature and who moved its status.

## Extensions

| Term      | URI                        |
| --------- | -------------------------- |
| `feature` | `org.dxos.mdl.feature@1.0` |

## Statuses

| Status        | Meaning                                              | Derived from the reviews when      |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `planned`     | Wanted; no work started.                             | every cited item is `missing`      |
| `in-progress` | Partly built: some of what it cites works, some not. | any mix that is not one of these   |
| `prototype`   | It works but is not relied on: nothing tests it.     | every item is `done` or `untested` |
| `operational` | It works and tests hold it.                          | every item is `done`               |
| `failing`     | It was operational and does not work now.            | never; a person or a QA run sets it |

An item with no verdict makes the derived status `unreviewed`, and the check compares nothing.

## Spec references

`specs:` lists repo-relative paths. A path alone cites every reviewable item of the spec; `#<id>`
cites one block, and a `feat` with `req` items stands for its requirements.

```text
packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl          # the whole spec
packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-1      # feat F-1, judged through F-1.1, F-1.2, ...
packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-2.3    # one requirement
packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#trim     # an op, type or component
```

## Checks

`deus features check` reports, per feature, `<id> <status> <derived> <done>/<items> done`, then:

- **errors** — an unknown status, a feature that cites no spec, a spec or item that does not exist,
  a `depends` on a feature not on the map, and a dependency cycle;
- **warnings** — a status the reviews do not support, a spec with no review or one that changed after
  its review's commit, a feature `blocked` by a planned or failing dependency, one `operational`
  ahead of a dependency, and an `in-progress` feature with no `## <feature id>` section in a
  `TASKS.md` beside its spec ("no work planned": a gap nobody has planned to close).

`deus features tasks <id>` prints the open items of one feature, each with its verdict and the first
sentence of the verdict's note, as the start of that `TASKS.md` section.

## Definitions

```mdl
ext feature
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feature@1.0
  desc: |
    A goal state of the product. The identifier is a stable kebab-case id, dotted by area
    (`echo.feed-retention`); the inline prose is the feature's name.
  fields:
    status: planned | in-progress | prototype | operational | failing
    specs: RefList          # repo-relative spec paths, each optionally #<item id>
    depends?: RefList       # ids of features this one needs first; the graph has no cycles
    note?: Prose            # why the feature matters, or what is left
  syntax: |
    feature echo.feed-retention: Feeds drop old entries
      status: planned
      specs: [packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-1, packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-2]
      depends: [echo.feed-store]
```
